## 3. Light technical check

**Method:** one HTTPS request to each homepage and one to each site's `robots.txt`, sent with a standard browser user-agent. No logins, no probing beyond that, and no attempt to get around any block. Run on 2026-10-29.

| Site | Homepage HTTP status | Bot blocking | `robots.txt` highlights |
|---|---|---|---|
| UWorld | 200 OK | None at the front door | Disallows demo pages, question-bank pages, `/terms.asp`, `/subscribe.asp` |
| AMBOSS | 302 redirect | None at the front door | Disallows `/api/`, `/search`, `/account/`, and `?format=json` variants |
| Figure 1 | **403 Forbidden** | Yes (Cloudflare-managed) | `Content-Signal: search=yes, ai-train=no`; blocks AI training crawlers |
| Radiopaedia | **406 Not Acceptable** | Yes (Cloudflare-managed) | `Content-Signal: search=yes, ai-train=no`; blocks AI training crawlers |
| VisualDx | 200 OK | None at the front door | Disallows `/articles/`, `/account/`, `/purchase/` |
| Sermo | **403 Forbidden** | Yes | Only disallows `/wp-admin/`; publishes a sitemap of its marketing pages |

### What this shows
- **Three of the six sites block simple automated clients before showing any content.**
- The three that load serve only **public marketing pages**. Their question banks, image libraries and analytics need a paid login.
- Figure 1 and Radiopaedia **explicitly reserve their rights against AI training** under Article 4 of the EU copyright directive (2019/790).
- **The answer to "would scraping work?" is no for all six**, unless the scraper either (a) gets around bot defenses or (b) collects data from inside an account. Both raise the legal problems below.

We deliberately did not try headless browsers, spoofed headers, rotating proxies or logged-in sessions. Each of those means actively getting around a site's protections.

