## 6. Legitimate alternatives

### 6.1 Licensing and partnerships
- **Radiopaedia** runs a licensing program aimed at commercial and AI use.
- **VisualDx** and **AMBOSS** have business-development and enterprise teams.
- **Sermo** sells physician polling and market research as a service.
- **UWorld** may discuss academic or research partnerships.
- Academic affiliation (CMU) may get better terms.

### 6.2 Open datasets by category

| Need (replaces) | Open alternatives |
|---|---|
| Board-style questions (UWorld, AMBOSS) | **MedQA** (USMLE), **MedMCQA**, **PubMedQA**, free **NBME/USMLE sample questions** |
| Radiology cases (Radiopaedia) | **MIMIC-CXR**, **CheXpert**, **NIH ChestX-ray14**, **RSNA challenge datasets**, **The Cancer Imaging Archive (TCIA)** |
| Dermatology images (VisualDx) | **ISIC Archive**, **HAM10000**, **Fitzpatrick17k**, **DDI** (Stanford), **SCIN** (Google) |
| Clinical case discussion (Figure 1) | **PMC Open Access case reports**, available through the NCBI API and licensed for reuse |
| Physician opinion (Sermo) | Published physician surveys; running our own panel (with IRB approval) |

Some datasets (e.g., MIMIC-CXR) require credentialed access and a data use agreement through PhysioNet. That's routine for academic users.

---

## 7. Recommendations

1. **Don't scrape** any of the six platforms.
2. **Define the end goal** (e.g., a medical QA model, a diagnostic imaging model, or market analysis) so we can match it to the right open datasets.
3. **Start with open datasets** for prototyping. They cover most of the same ground.
4. **Contact licensing teams** (Radiopaedia, VisualDx, AMBOSS, Sermo) if proprietary data turns out to be essential.
5. **Involve CMU legal and the IRB** early for any work touching patient images or physician communications.
